Add continuous integration to this repository with GitHub Actions (or improve the existing workflow if there is one).

1. Find the commands a developer runs: install, build, type-check, lint, format check and tests, from the README, package scripts, Makefile or similar. Run each one locally first and note which pass today. A workflow that fails on day one helps nobody: if a check fails now, keep it out of the required job and list it for me.
2. Write `.github/workflows/ci.yml`:
   - triggers on `push` to the default branch and on `pull_request`;
   - `permissions: contents: read` unless a step needs more;
   - pinned runtime versions that match the project (read `.nvmrc`, `.python-version`, `go.mod`, `rust-toolchain`, `engines` and the like);
   - install from the lock file (`npm ci`, `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`, `pip install -r` with hashes or `uv sync --frozen`, and so on) with the setup action's built-in caching;
   - one job per concern only if that makes it faster or clearer; a matrix only for versions or platforms the project claims to support;
   - `concurrency` that cancels superseded runs on the same branch;
   - a timeout on each job.
3. Use only well-known actions and pin them to a major version tag at least; never add a step that sends code or secrets to a third-party service.
4. Validate the YAML (for example `actionlint` if available) and, if `act` is installed, run the workflow locally.

Do not push. Report the workflow, the commands it runs, the checks you left out and why, and the expected run time.

Add contract tests for this project's HTTP API, so that a change that would break clients fails the test suite.

1. List the endpoints from the router or the OpenAPI spec if there is one. For each, note the method, path, auth requirement, request body, success responses and error responses. If there is an OpenAPI or JSON Schema document, check it against the code and list where they disagree.
2. Use the project's existing test framework and HTTP test helpers (for example `supertest`, the framework's test client, `httptest`). Run the app in-process against a test database with seeded data, not against a deployed server.
3. For each endpoint, test:
   - the success case: status code, content type, and the full shape of the body (field names, types, required versus optional, nullability), using a schema assertion if the project has one or explicit assertions if not;
   - authentication and authorisation: no credentials, wrong user, and the right user;
   - validation errors: status code and the error body format;
   - not found and conflict cases where they exist;
   - pagination, sorting and filtering parameters, including their limits.
4. Assert on what clients depend on, not on incidental details such as generated IDs, timestamps or field order.
5. If the spec and the code disagree, do not silently pick one: write the test for the current behaviour, mark the disagreement in a comment, and list it in your report.

Run the full suite and make sure the new tests pass. Then prove they guard the contract: rename one response field temporarily, run the tests, see them fail, and restore it. Report endpoints covered, tests added, spec mismatches, and endpoints you could not cover with the reason.

Turn the TODO comments in this repository into well-written issue drafts. Do not create any issue and do not edit source files.

1. Search tracked files for `TODO`, `FIXME`, `HACK`, `XXX` and `@todo` (for example `git grep -n -E "TODO|FIXME|HACK|XXX"`), skipping vendored and generated folders. For each hit, record the file, line, the comment, and `git blame` for its author and date.
2. Read the code around each one so you understand what is actually missing. Drop comments that are already done (the code does what the TODO asks), and say which ones and why.
3. Group TODOs that describe the same work into one issue. Check open issues (`gh issue list --state open --limit 200` if `gh` is available) and mark drafts that duplicate an existing issue instead of drafting them again.
4. Write `TODO-ISSUES.md` with one section per draft issue:
   - a title in sentence case that says the outcome;
   - what is wrong or missing today, and why it matters, in two to four sentences;
   - links to the exact lines (`path:line`);
   - a suggested label (bug, enhancement, tech debt, docs) and a size (small, medium, large);
   - what done looks like, as a checklist.
5. At the end of the file, add a shell script block that would create each issue with `gh issue create --title ... --body-file ...`, so I can run it after reviewing. Do not run it.

Report how many TODOs you found, how many were already done, how many drafts you wrote, and the five you think matter most.

Write a CSV parser and writer as a standalone JavaScript module. This task is self-contained: it works in any git repository, an empty one included, and needs only Node.js 20 or later.

Create `kata/csv/csv.mjs` exporting two functions, with no dependencies:

- `parseCSV(text, { delimiter = ",", header = false } = {})` follows RFC 4180:
  - Rows end with `\n`, `\r\n` or a lone `\r`. A final line ending does not create an extra row; an empty string gives `[]`. An empty line in the middle is a row with one empty field.
  - Fields may be quoted with `"`. Inside quotes, `""` is a literal quote, and delimiters and line endings are data.
  - Whitespace is kept as data. A leading byte order mark is dropped.
  - Errors are `SyntaxError`s whose message names the line number (1-based, counting physical lines): an unterminated quote names the line where the quoted field started; a quote inside an unquoted field and characters after a closing quote are errors too.
  - Without `header`, return an array of arrays of strings. With `header: true`, use the first row as keys and return an array of objects; a row with a different number of fields is a `SyntaxError` that names its line.
  - It must run in linear time: no re-scanning, no quadratic string building.
- `stringifyCSV(rows, { delimiter = "," } = {})` writes rows of values, ending every row with `\r\n`. `null` and `undefined` become empty fields; other values go through `String`. Quote a field only when it contains the delimiter, a quote, `\r` or `\n`, doubling the quotes inside.

Create `kata/csv/acceptance.test.mjs` with exactly the content below and do not change it. You may add more tests in other files in the same folder, for example `kata/csv/extra.test.mjs`.

```js
import { test } from "node:test";
import assert from "node:assert/strict";
import { parseCSV, stringifyCSV } from "./csv.mjs";

test("plain rows", () => {
  assert.deepEqual(parseCSV("a,b,c\n1,2,3"), [["a", "b", "c"], ["1", "2", "3"]]);
});

test("a trailing newline does not add a row; an empty input has no rows", () => {
  assert.deepEqual(parseCSV("a,b\n1,2\n"), [["a", "b"], ["1", "2"]]);
  assert.deepEqual(parseCSV(""), []);
});

test("CRLF and lone CR line endings", () => {
  assert.deepEqual(parseCSV("a,b\r\n1,2\r\n"), [["a", "b"], ["1", "2"]]);
  assert.deepEqual(parseCSV("a,b\r1,2"), [["a", "b"], ["1", "2"]]);
});

test("empty fields are kept", () => {
  assert.deepEqual(parseCSV(",a,,\n"), [["", "a", "", ""]]);
  assert.deepEqual(parseCSV("a\n\nb"), [["a"], [""], ["b"]]);
});

test("quoted fields with delimiters, doubled quotes and newlines", () => {
  const text = 'name,quote\n"Smith, J.","He said ""hi"""\n"multi\nline",x\n';
  assert.deepEqual(parseCSV(text), [
    ["name", "quote"],
    ["Smith, J.", 'He said "hi"'],
    ["multi\nline", "x"],
  ]);
});

test("whitespace is data, not trimmed", () => {
  assert.deepEqual(parseCSV(" a , b "), [[" a ", " b "]]);
});

test("custom delimiter", () => {
  assert.deepEqual(parseCSV('a;"b;c"\n1;2', { delimiter: ";" }), [["a", "b;c"], ["1", "2"]]);
  assert.deepEqual(parseCSV("a\tb", { delimiter: "\t" }), [["a", "b"]]);
});

test("header mode returns objects", () => {
  assert.deepEqual(parseCSV("id,name\n1,Ada\n2,Linus\n", { header: true }), [
    { id: "1", name: "Ada" },
    { id: "2", name: "Linus" },
  ]);
});

test("header mode rejects rows with the wrong number of fields", () => {
  assert.throws(() => parseCSV("id,name\n1\n", { header: true }), (err) => err instanceof SyntaxError && /line 2/.test(err.message));
});

test("an unterminated quote is a SyntaxError that names the line it started on", () => {
  assert.throws(() => parseCSV('a,b\n1,"oops\n2,3\n'), (err) => err instanceof SyntaxError && /line 2/.test(err.message));
});

test("a quote in the middle of an unquoted field is a SyntaxError", () => {
  assert.throws(() => parseCSV('a,b"c\n'), SyntaxError);
});

test("characters after a closing quote are a SyntaxError", () => {
  assert.throws(() => parseCSV('"a"b,c\n'), SyntaxError);
});

test("unicode is preserved", () => {
  assert.deepEqual(parseCSV("şehir,emoji\nİstanbul,🚀"), [["şehir", "emoji"], ["İstanbul", "🚀"]]);
});

test("a byte order mark at the start is dropped", () => {
  assert.deepEqual(parseCSV("﻿a,b"), [["a", "b"]]);
});

test("stringify quotes only when needed and ends every row with CRLF", () => {
  assert.equal(stringifyCSV([["a", "b,c"], ['say "hi"', "line\nbreak"], ["", "plain"]]), 'a,"b,c"\r\n"say ""hi""","line\nbreak"\r\n,plain\r\n');
});

test("stringify accepts numbers, booleans and null", () => {
  assert.equal(stringifyCSV([[1, true, null, undefined, 2.5]]), "1,true,,,2.5\r\n");
});

test("round trip", () => {
  const rows = [["id", "note"], ["1", 'a "quoted", multi\r\nline value'], ["2", ""], ["3", " spaced "]];
  assert.deepEqual(parseCSV(stringifyCSV(rows)), rows);
  assert.deepEqual(parseCSV(stringifyCSV(rows, { delimiter: ";" }), { delimiter: ";" }), rows);
});

test("parses a large file in linear time", () => {
  const line = '1,"two, with comma",three,"four ""quoted"""\n';
  const text = line.repeat(100_000);
  const start = performance.now();
  const rows = parseCSV(text);
  assert.equal(rows.length, 100_000);
  assert.deepEqual(rows[99_999], ["1", "two, with comma", "three", 'four "quoted"']);
  assert.ok(performance.now() - start < 3000, "100k rows should parse well under 3 seconds");
});
```

Done means `node --test kata/csv/*.test.mjs` passes. Run it, and include the output summary in your final message together with any edge case you handled beyond the tests. Then commit your work as the instructions below say.

Implement an LRU cache with expiry as a standalone JavaScript module. This task is self-contained: it works in any git repository, an empty one included, and needs only Node.js 20 or later.

Create `kata/lru-cache/lru-cache.mjs` exporting `class LRUCache`:

- `new LRUCache({ max, ttlMs = 0, now = Date.now, onEvict })`. `max` must be a positive integer, otherwise throw a `RangeError`. `ttlMs` is the default time to live in milliseconds; `0` means entries never expire. `now` is the clock, so tests can control time. `onEvict(key, value)` is optional.
- `get(key)` returns the value or `undefined`, and marks the entry as most recently used. It does not extend the expiry.
- `set(key, value, { ttlMs } = {})` stores the value as most recently used, with a per-entry ttl that overrides the default (`0` means never expire), and restarts the expiry. Setting an existing key replaces it without growing the cache. When the cache is over `max`, first drop expired entries, then evict least recently used entries; call `onEvict` only for these capacity evictions, not for `delete`, overwrites or expiry.
- `has(key)` says whether a live entry exists, without changing recency.
- `delete(key)` returns whether something was removed; `clear()` empties the cache.
- `size` (a getter) and `keys()` (most recently used first) count only live entries.
- `get`, `set`, `has` and `delete` must be O(1) on average. No dependencies.

Create `kata/lru-cache/acceptance.test.mjs` with exactly the content below and do not change it. You may add more tests in other files in the same folder, for example `kata/lru-cache/extra.test.mjs`.

```js
import { test } from "node:test";
import assert from "node:assert/strict";
import { LRUCache } from "./lru-cache.mjs";

function clock(start = 0) {
  let t = start;
  const now = () => t;
  now.advance = (ms) => { t += ms; };
  return now;
}

test("rejects a capacity that is not a positive integer", () => {
  for (const max of [0, -1, 1.5, NaN, "3", undefined]) {
    assert.throws(() => new LRUCache({ max }), RangeError);
  }
});

test("evicts the least recently used entry", () => {
  const c = new LRUCache({ max: 2 });
  c.set("a", 1);
  c.set("b", 2);
  c.set("c", 3);
  assert.equal(c.get("a"), undefined);
  assert.equal(c.get("b"), 2);
  assert.equal(c.get("c"), 3);
  assert.equal(c.size, 2);
});

test("get refreshes recency, has does not", () => {
  const c = new LRUCache({ max: 2 });
  c.set("a", 1);
  c.set("b", 2);
  c.get("a");
  c.set("c", 3);
  assert.equal(c.has("a"), true);
  assert.equal(c.has("b"), false);
  c.has("a");
  c.set("d", 4);
  assert.equal(c.has("a"), false);
  assert.equal(c.has("c"), true);
});

test("set on an existing key updates the value and recency without growing", () => {
  const c = new LRUCache({ max: 2 });
  c.set("a", 1);
  c.set("b", 2);
  c.set("a", 10);
  c.set("c", 3);
  assert.equal(c.size, 2);
  assert.equal(c.get("a"), 10);
  assert.equal(c.get("b"), undefined);
});

test("keys() lists most recently used first", () => {
  const c = new LRUCache({ max: 3 });
  c.set("a", 1);
  c.set("b", 2);
  c.set("c", 3);
  c.get("a");
  assert.deepEqual([...c.keys()], ["a", "c", "b"]);
});

test("stores falsy values and distinguishes them from missing keys", () => {
  const c = new LRUCache({ max: 5 });
  c.set("zero", 0);
  c.set("empty", "");
  c.set("nothing", null);
  assert.equal(c.get("zero"), 0);
  assert.equal(c.get("empty"), "");
  assert.equal(c.get("nothing"), null);
  assert.equal(c.has("nothing"), true);
  assert.equal(c.has("missing"), false);
});

test("delete and clear", () => {
  const c = new LRUCache({ max: 3 });
  c.set("a", 1);
  c.set("b", 2);
  assert.equal(c.delete("a"), true);
  assert.equal(c.delete("a"), false);
  assert.equal(c.size, 1);
  c.clear();
  assert.equal(c.size, 0);
  assert.deepEqual([...c.keys()], []);
});

test("entries expire after the default ttl", () => {
  const now = clock();
  const c = new LRUCache({ max: 3, ttlMs: 100, now });
  c.set("a", 1);
  now.advance(99);
  assert.equal(c.get("a"), 1);
  now.advance(1);
  assert.equal(c.get("a"), undefined);
  assert.equal(c.has("a"), false);
  assert.equal(c.size, 0);
});

test("a per-entry ttl overrides the default, and 0 means no expiry", () => {
  const now = clock();
  const c = new LRUCache({ max: 3, ttlMs: 100, now });
  c.set("short", 1, { ttlMs: 10 });
  c.set("forever", 2, { ttlMs: 0 });
  c.set("default", 3);
  now.advance(50);
  assert.deepEqual([...c.keys()].sort(), ["default", "forever"]);
  now.advance(1000);
  assert.deepEqual([...c.keys()], ["forever"]);
  assert.equal(c.size, 1);
});

test("get does not extend the ttl, set does", () => {
  const now = clock();
  const c = new LRUCache({ max: 3, ttlMs: 100, now });
  c.set("a", 1);
  now.advance(60);
  c.get("a");
  now.advance(60);
  assert.equal(c.get("a"), undefined);
  c.set("b", 1);
  now.advance(60);
  c.set("b", 2);
  now.advance(60);
  assert.equal(c.get("b"), 2);
});

test("expired entries do not cause live ones to be evicted", () => {
  const now = clock();
  const c = new LRUCache({ max: 2, ttlMs: 0, now });
  c.set("old", 1, { ttlMs: 10 });
  c.set("live", 2);
  now.advance(20);
  c.set("new", 3);
  assert.equal(c.get("live"), 2);
  assert.equal(c.get("new"), 3);
});

test("onEvict is called for capacity evictions but not for delete or overwrite", () => {
  const evicted = [];
  const c = new LRUCache({ max: 1, onEvict: (key, value) => evicted.push([key, value]) });
  c.set("a", 1);
  c.set("a", 2);
  c.delete("a");
  c.set("b", 3);
  c.set("c", 4);
  assert.deepEqual(evicted, [["b", 3]]);
});

test("handles many operations quickly", () => {
  const c = new LRUCache({ max: 1000 });
  const start = performance.now();
  for (let i = 0; i < 200_000; i++) {
    c.set(i % 5000, i);
    c.get((i * 7) % 5000);
  }
  assert.equal(c.size, 1000);
  assert.ok(performance.now() - start < 2000, "200k set+get pairs should take well under 2 seconds");
});
```

Done means `node --test kata/lru-cache/*.test.mjs` passes. Run it, and include the output summary in your final message together with a short note on the data structure you chose and why. Then commit your work as the instructions below say.
